      "a9a9b172",
      "Homepage Builder / web framework prototype design",
      "I want you to atomically break down the app and separate concerns. You will need to import my core and validators from my BEJSON libraries. \n\nI want you to build two new family Libraries for Python.\n\nWeb_Framework, which one basically have a pre scaffolded database that contains the raw template barebones shell of this app maintaining its style but no details or sections it will just be a very clean template file skeleton along with the CSS and two separate entities A library function will deploy this barenlbones framework with its core web framework library file... That's all we will do with it for now. \n\nThe other library will be called Management, and we will take each section of this app the notes the task manager the links and will create individual files for each of these sections giving each section a database of their own and basically breaking this section down atomically separating concerns into as many files as possible to keep file size blow down from any individual file. As we build this app the management library will grow for now the framework library is just a starting point for a separate project. \n\n\nnew Web_Framework library that will be entirely separate from the HTML3 libraries and they should never touch each other. This is going to be something much better. \n\nWon't make the same mistake I did with the HTML3 library but making a bunch of page and few times and making it a manager.... \n\nThe core architecture will have a taxonomy entity for registering taxonomy types... Each type of taxonomy will have a templated mfdb of its own it won't have to have the same exact Fields but the core Fields will do the same... Something like...\n\n# COMMON MFDB ENGITY TAXONOMY SCHEMA\n\n\nTAXONOMY: note, task, link, PAGE, ETC\nTYPE: (OPTIONAL) \nCATEGORY: \nID:\nLABEL:\nIDX_POSITION: #\nIDX_SORTING: Default/date/optional\nActive:\nHidden:\n\n\n/Content//Notes\n/Content/Notes/MFDB\n\n\n/Content/Tasks\n/Content/Tasks/MFDB1 \n\n/Content/Links\n/Content/Links/DB\n\n/Content/Tasks\n/Content/Tasks/MFDB\n\n\nFIELDS IN EACH INDIVIDUAL TAXONY MANIFEST WILL MIMIC ALL THE RECORDS IN THE MAIN MFDB ENTITY LINKING THEM \n\n\nAFTER PAGE DESIGN WE'RE GOING TO HAVE A VERY SIMPLE TEMPLATE YOU'LL BE STORED IN THE CORE AND MFDB AND IT TEMPLATES ENTITY, WITH A KEY LINKING TO IT'S CSS ALSO EMBEDDED IN THE MFDB. WHEN THE PYTHON CALLS TO PRODUCE THE TEMPLATE YOU'LL PRODUCE THE APPROPRIATE CSS AND JAVASCRIPT WITH IT ALL LINKED BY ID \n.\nDON'T HAVE A MILLION PAGE TYPES THIS TIME WE MIGHT HAVE SOME COMPONENTS BUT WE'RE GOING TO DO THIS RIGHT \n\nTHE NEW WEB FRAMEWORK LIBRARY WILL LAY THIS OUT GOING FORWARD IT'LL BE HOW WE REGISTER A LOT OF STUFF DIFFERENT APPS\nThis is just a rough draft but we will break down each section and componentize it separating concerns into each section and we will also base the web framework library off of this scaffold concept that it will basically be able to deploy structure with a database rebuild with the core fields ready to go to properly componentize everything off whatever design mean decided upon.\n\nThe management Library will be actually what contains the actual components and functionality of this have itself so the framework is going to be groundwork to repeat this copponentized architecture off of however we build this at its core but the management library will be ultimately what controls the specific app and all of its features. \n\nHope that makes sense but basically using this app to set up a new web framework but we're setting up the management library to actually manage this more app and keep its logic contains within us each individual sections and managing the debate is that the framework actually would be deploying and they will be completely independent of each other This will probably be the only app to ever encounter each other",
